# PlayerTrace clang-tidy configuration.
#
# Scope: CORRECTNESS. This selects the checks that find real defects — the
# static analyzer, bug-prone patterns, portability, CERT rules, and the
# performance checks whose findings are genuine rather than cosmetic.
#
# Whole families are deliberately NOT enabled. `modernize-*` and `readability-*`
# would rewrite the established C++17 public API for style alone — adding
# [[nodiscard]] to every accessor, braced-init returns, nested-namespace
# collapsing, literal-suffix casing — several hundred findings with no defect
# among them, and a diff large enough to bury anything real in review. A rule
# that cannot honestly fail a build does not belong in a gate that treats
# warnings as errors, and formatting is already enforced by clang-format.
#
# third_party/ is excluded by HeaderFilterRegex and by the runner's file list.
---
Checks: >
  clang-analyzer-*,
  bugprone-*,
  portability-*,
  performance-*,
  cert-*,
  misc-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-slicing,
  cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-static-cast-downcast,
  cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-empty-catch,
  -bugprone-branch-clone,
  -bugprone-unused-local-non-trivial-variable,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-const-correctness,
  -misc-use-internal-linkage,
  -performance-enum-size,
  -performance-unnecessary-value-param,
  -performance-no-int-to-ptr,
  -performance-trivially-destructible,
  -cert-err58-cpp,
  -cert-dcl21-cpp

# Each suppression above is justified here, so none of them is a silent shrug.
#
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#   Fires on ordinary (name, value) and (timeout, out) pairs across the API.
#   Renaming or wrapping them changes the public surface and fixes no defect.
#
# bugprone-empty-catch
#   PlayerTrace's exception barriers are deliberately empty: a throwing user
#   log/error callback, a throwing sink cancel, and the worker's outermost
#   containment barrier must swallow rather than propagate, because there is no
#   safe place to rethrow and the alternative is terminating the host game. Each
#   site carries a comment saying so. This is the documented design — see
#   docs/reliability.md — not a hidden failure.
#
# bugprone-branch-clone
#   Matches on branch SHAPE, not meaning. It fires where two arms are each a
#   single call — session_.deactivate() versus session_.restore(previous), and
#   terminal_status_ versus flush_result_locked(token) — which are opposite
#   behaviours. Collapsing either would reintroduce a fixed defect.
#
# bugprone-unused-local-non-trivial-variable
#   Does not count a variable whose ADDRESS is passed to an out-parameter as
#   used, so it flags every `std::string error; if (!f(..., &error))` pair —
#   the project's standard way of returning a diagnostic message alongside a
#   bool. The variables are read immediately afterwards.
#
# cert-err33-c
#   Flags unchecked returns from C calls that are deliberately best-effort:
#   fflush/fclose during teardown and rollback, where the failure has already
#   been recorded and there is nothing further the code could do. The paths
#   that DO matter — the durable write, sync and truncate — are all checked.
#
# misc-const-correctness
#   Local-variable advice only, dozens of findings, no defect among them. It
#   also reads badly on RAII guards, where `const StmtGuard guard(stmt)`
#   suggests the object does nothing.
#
# misc-use-internal-linkage
#   Misidentifies out-of-line MEMBER function definitions (WorkerCore::run and
#   friends) as file-local "variables" that could be made static. They cannot;
#   the check is simply wrong here.
#
# performance-unnecessary-value-param
#   The single finding is Client::track(std::string event_name,
#   Properties properties) — a PUBLIC signature, and a deliberate one. Taking
#   both by value lets a caller move into the call
#   (client->track(std::move(name), std::move(props))) and pay nothing, which is
#   the common path in a game loop. Switching to const& would forbid that and
#   force a copy for every caller who already owns a temporary. It is also an
#   API break for anyone who has already compiled against it. The check cannot
#   see either consideration, so it is disabled here rather than obeyed — the
#   parameter is not unnecessary, it is the interface.
#
# performance-no-int-to-ptr
#   Every hit is SQLite's own SQLITE_TRANSIENT, which is defined as
#   ((sqlite3_destructor_type)-1). It is the documented way to call
#   sqlite3_bind_text and cannot be written any other way.
#
# performance-trivially-destructible
#   Wants ~Client() defaulted at its first declaration. It cannot be: Client is
#   a pimpl and Impl is incomplete in the header, so an inline destructor would
#   not compile.
#
# misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes
#   Fires on the plain data-carrying types (Event, Config, EventBatch) whose
#   public members ARE the API.
#
# misc-no-recursion
#   Flags recursive descent that is correct and bounded.
#
# misc-include-cleaner
#   Demands every transitive include be spelled out per translation unit; noisy,
#   and it fights the deliberate internal/ header layering.
#
# performance-enum-size
#   Suggests narrowing the underlying type of public enums (ErrorCode,
#   ConsentState, LogLevel). That is an ABI-visible change to save a few bytes.
#
# cert-err58-cpp
#   Fires on static objects with non-trivial constructors in the vendored Catch2
#   registration path, not on first-party code.
#
# cert-dcl21-cpp
#   Postfix-operator return-const advice, obsolete under C++17 move semantics.

WarningsAsErrors: '*'

# Cover every first-party header, including the internal ones under src/internal
# (an earlier pattern required a "playertrace" path segment, so those were
# silently excluded). third_party/ contains no include/ or src/ segment and is
# therefore still skipped.
HeaderFilterRegex: '^.*[/\\](include|src)[/\\].*'
FormatStyle: file
